//! # Resources
//!
//! `#[resource]` marks an owned external handle. Its sibling `impl` declares a
//! consuming `close` method (written `fn close(value: Type)` in the current
//! surface; conceptually `close(self)`); the compiler invokes
//! that release path at scope exit and discards its `Result`. A value is
//! closed exactly once, including when it is moved through a return or actor
//! boundary. Early close consumes the value, so a later implicit close cannot
//! happen.
//!
//! ```hew
//! #[resource]
//! type Connection {
//!     id: i64
//! }
//!
//! impl Connection {
//!     fn close(connection: Connection) {
//!         print(connection.id);
//!         println("");
//!     }
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     let connection = Connection { id: 7 };
//!     println("working");
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! Resource cleanup is deterministic and field-wise cleanup is reverse
//! declaration order. `impl Drop for T` is refused: user-defined Drop is not
//! a cleanup hook in Hew. A resource close method must be in a sibling impl,
//! not inline in the type body.
//!
//! Resource values cannot escape their ownership model. The checker refuses
//! duplicated use after move, conditional paths that close only some branches,
//! storing a resource in a machine state, and passing an opaque handle as a
//! receive parameter. Use an owned typed PID or a plain serializable value at
//! those boundaries instead.
